Register allocation: Linear scan

Reserved registers: 
	eip: Instruction pointer
	ebp: Function frame base pointer (this is where the memory for each function call starts)
	esp: Stack pointer 
	esi and edi: We won't use them, used in string operations and sometimes general purpose

Available registers: eax, ebx, ecx, edx

Return value register: eax

Spill register: eax

Registers to be preserved by the caller: eax, ecx, edx (push on to the stack before call and pop after the call)
Registers preserved by the callee: ebx (all others)

Parameter passing:
Caller: pushes all parameters on to the stack from last to first
Callee: copies all the parameters from the caller's stack frame to local copy


Understanding stack: 
	relevant registers: ebp and esp 

What do leave, call and return do:
	leave: Restores the value of ebp and esp to what they were at the 
				 start of the function 
	call: 1) Copies return address (instruction after call)
				2) Moves eip to function that is being called
	return:	Sets eip to return address
[Special note: some registers have to be preserved for example: ebx]


Important note: We will be creating 32-bit assembly code, so remember to
compile final assembly code and main.c with -m32 flag.
